  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Constellation of Courts is showing clear early use through 2,902 recorded downloads, suggesting sustained readership for a scholarly monograph that connects Habsburg courtly communities across the traditional Spanish-Austrian divide. Its open access availability through a hybrid model likely supports this reach, while its alignment with Peace, Justice and strong institutions indicates relevance beyond purely historiographical debates.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This book brings together Ren\u00e9 Vermeir, Dries Raeymaekers, and Jos\u00e9 Eloy Hortal Mu\u00f1oz in a study of Habsburg courts and households after the dynastic division under Charles V. Its main scholarly contribution lies in rethinking court history as an interconnected network rather than as separate national traditions, a framing that speaks directly to historiography, political history, and the study of institutions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is use: the book has been downloaded 2,902 times in the available metrics, with monthly activity showing recurring readership and a pronounced spike in one month. That pattern points to active consultation rather than a one-time release effect. The record does not yet show citation-based uptake, so the scholarly afterlife remains early in bibliometric terms, but the usage evidence shows that the work is already circulating.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Open access availability is also a meaningful part of its reach. The record shows one open instance, which lowers access barriers for readers and helps explain why the book is able to accumulate downloads. The peer-review signal from the publisher further supports the book\u2019s credibility as a formal scholarly contribution.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The thematic profile reinforces its broader relevance. The book is associated with Peace, Justice and strong institutions, a signal that fits its focus on governance, dynastic order, and institutional relationships in early modern Europe. At present, the public and event record is sparse, so the evidence for impact is concentrated in access and use rather than in media attention or documented events. Even so, the available record shows a book that is being read and that addresses questions with continuing value for historians and scholars of institutions.</p>",
